1 0 0 1 46 443 Tm
(A simple explanation:) Tj
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 58 428 Tm
(1. I started with a rough Expo app and real errors.) Tj
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 58 413 Tm
(2. I used AI like a coding partner, not a magic button.) Tj
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 58 398 Tm
(3. I gave it bugs, screenshots, UI feelings, and product direction.) Tj
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 58 383 Tm
(4. It inspected the code, changed files, and ran checks.) Tj
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 58 368 Tm
(5. I tested on my phone and reported what felt wrong.) Tj
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 58 353 Tm
(6. We repeated until it felt like a real app.) Tj
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 58 338 Tm
(7. Native features still needed real builds, installs, and QA.) Tj
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 58 323 Tm
(8. The final product came from taste plus iteration, not one giant prompt.) Tj
/F1 15 Tf
1 0 0 1 46 292 Tm
(Main Lesson) Tj
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 46 267 Tm
(Vibecoding worked best when the user stayed involved as product owner, tester, and) Tj
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 46 252 Tm
(taste-maker.) Tj
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 46 237 Tm
(The assistant was strongest at fast implementation, debugging, consistency checks, and) Tj
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 46 222 Tm
(explaining tradeoffs.) Tj
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 46 207 Tm
(The user was strongest at deciding what felt good, what felt unnecessary, and what the app) Tj
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 46 192 Tm
(should become.) Tj
/F1 11 Tf
1 0 0 1 46 169 Tm
(That combination is the real workflow: human judgment steering AI implementation.) Tj
